### Declare a slot with `$slot` and fill it with `$in`

A wrapper can give part of its content a name. Declare that part with `$slot`
and supply it from the caller with `$in`. A slot without a name receives the
remaining content.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    card #= [$html:<h2>[$slot title = "Draft"]</h2><p>[$slot]</p>]
    notice = [card:[$in title:Build]All tests passed.]
]

The result contains `<h2>Build</h2><p>All tests passed.</p>`. If you leave
out the title insert, the heading reads `Draft`. An explicitly empty title
insert leaves the heading empty.

### Test for a contribution with `$supplied`

Use a presence guard when a caller can choose whether a piece of output
appears. The guard can test a name even when no slot prints its value.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    button #= [$html:<button[$supplied disabled:[" disabled"]]>[$slot]</button>]
    active = [button:Save]
    inactive = [button:[$in disabled:]Save]
]

Both buttons read `Save`. Only the second has the `disabled` attribute.
Its empty insert still counts. The guard body supplies the leading space
before the attribute name.

### Supply an HTML page title

A direct insert at the top of an active `@entry.moth` sends a compile-time
value to the selected builder. The HTML builder accepts a `title` destination.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    [$in title:Build report]
]

Inside a card, `title` names the card's slot. Here it sets the page title.
